WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.580 --> 00:00:04.620
Mighty Marvel may be dominating pop culture,
but not everyone is feeling the love.

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:09.430
In a recent conversation with Variety, Jennifer
Aniston star of the beloved sitcom Friends

00:00:09.430 --> 00:00:14.080
and the upcoming Apple TV+ series The Morning
Show lamented what she saw as a dearth of

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:17.460
cinematic choices due to the popularity of
comic book movies.

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:22.240
It was a rather hard segue in an interview
which had, up until that point, been focused

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:25.960
on The Morning Show and how it had been shaped,
in part, by the "Me Too" movement.

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:30.420
Aniston was asked a simple question about
what prompted her return to television fifteen

00:00:30.439 --> 00:00:34.870
years after shooting her last episode of Friends,
and in her response, she veered quickly from

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:38.890
marveling about all of the awesome choices
available to viewers since the advent of streaming

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:41.860
to… well, not marveling about Marvel.

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:42.400
She said,

00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:45.960
"And then you're seeing what's available out
there, and it's just diminishing and diminishing

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:48.140
in terms of, it's big Marvel movies.

00:00:48.350 --> 00:00:52.100
Or things that I'm not just asked to do […] [I'm
not] really that interested in living in [front

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:53.100
of] a green screen."

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:57.320
Well, we suppose it's kind of a bummer when
you're an actress who is well-known for starring

00:00:57.320 --> 00:01:01.219
on one of the most popular sitcoms in the
history of television, and you then find that

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:05.420
your agent is always trying to talk you into
taking roles in these silly comic book pictures,

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:09.659
where you'll be forced to report to work and
pretend to talk to a gun-toting CGI raccoon

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:10.160
all day.

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:13.220
"Ha ha ha ha."

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:14.240
"That's a fake laugh."

00:01:14.360 --> 00:01:15.260
"It's real!"

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:19.679
The interviewer responded to this unprompted
diss by noting how much Hollywood has changed

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:21.890
in recent years, to which Aniston replied,

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:23.260
"It's changed so much.

00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:26.420
I think we would so love to have the era of
Meg Ryan come back.

00:01:26.660 --> 00:01:29.440
I just think it would be nice to go into a
movie theater, sit cozy.

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:31.500
I think we should have a resurgence.

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:33.420
Let's get the Terms of Endearment back out
there.

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:37.520
You know, Heaven Can Wait, Young Frankenstein,
Blazing Saddles, Goodbye Girl."

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:42.039
It seems to have become somewhat fashionable
to trash the Marvel machine as of late, however

00:01:42.039 --> 00:01:46.219
so, while it might seem a little out of left
field for Aniston to cite Marvel movies as

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:49.720
the reason why she's choosing to continue
working in television, she's certainly not

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:50.220
alone.

00:01:50.340 --> 00:01:54.739
Most recently, Martin Scorsese made headlines
for blasting comic book movies in general,

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:56.440
and in far more direct fashion.

00:01:56.840 --> 00:02:00.619
When asked if he had kept up with the latest
releases in the superhero genre, the filmmaker

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:01.500
flatly stated,

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:02.700
"That's not cinema.

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:06.639
Honestly, the closest I can think of them,
as well made as they are, with actors doing

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:09.600
the best they can under the circumstances,
is theme parks.

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:13.980
It isn't the cinema of human beings trying
to convey emotional, psychological experiences

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:15.220
to another human being."

00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:19.680
Scorsese isn't the only acclaimed filmmaker
to make the dubious comparison between comic

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:21.260
book films and theme parks.

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:26.819
In a 2018 conversation with Radio Times magazine,
Jodie Foster unloaded on the entirety of the

00:02:26.819 --> 00:02:31.340
superhero genre, going so far as to say that
such films pollute the cinematic landscape

00:02:31.340 --> 00:02:34.880
in much the same way that poor environmental
practices pollute the planet.

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:35.740
Foster said,

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:38.260
"Going to the movies has become like a theme
park.

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:43.530
Studios making bad content in order to appeal
to the masses and shareholders is like fracking

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:46.040
you get the best return right now, but you
wreck the earth.

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:50.170
It's ruining the viewing habits of the American
population, and then ultimately, the rest

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:50.780
of the world."

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:55.140
The comic book movie backlash, while nothing
new, does appear to be picking up a little

00:02:55.140 --> 00:02:55.640
steam.

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:59.300
Perhaps it has something to do with the fact
that Avengers: Endgame recently became the

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:03.780
highest-grossing film of all time, or that
the entire world briefly lost their minds

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:08.370
when Spider-Man was temporarily evicted from
the Marvel Cinematic Universe, or that Joker

00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:10.360
has become the most talked-about film of the
fall.

00:03:10.709 --> 00:03:15.360
We submit, however, that a sizable market
for non-comic book fare still exists, and

00:03:15.370 --> 00:03:19.489
that if actors like Aniston and filmmakers
like Scorsese don't want to work in the genre,

00:03:19.489 --> 00:03:20.800
nobody is going to force them to.

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:24.080
Heck, we hear that Scorsese just made his
best film in years.

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:28.100
It's called The Irishman, and it's not even
about a guy flying around in a green suit,

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:31.140
defeating his enemies by pelting them with
explosive shamrocks.

